      "raw": "In each of the three views, we see the same three garments: a maroon sweater, a grey sweater, and black-and-white checkered pants. The garments are arranged differently across the views, but their visual characteristics \u2014 fabric, color, construction \u2014 remain consistent.\n\nWe are given mask regions for each view:\n\n- **VIEW control_01_all_present_v0**: R001 (maroon sweater), R002 (grey sweater), R003 (checkered pants)\n- **VIEW control_01_all_present_v1**: R004 (maroon sweater), R005 (grey sweater), R006 (checkered pants)\n- **VIEW control_01_all_present_v2**: R007 (maroon sweater), R008 (grey sweater), R009 (checkered pants)\n\nEach region corresponds to one of the three garments, and the same garment appears in every view, just with different region IDs.\n\nWe must assign each region to a garment track, ensuring that each track includes **at least one region from every view**. Since each garment appears in every view, we can form three tracks:\n\n1. **Maroon sweater**: Appears in R001 (v0), R004 (v1), R007 (v2)\n2. **Grey sweater**: Appears in R002 (v0), R005 (v1), R008 (v2)\n3. **Checkered pants**: Appears in R003 (v0), R006 (v1), R009 (v2)\n\nNo region is shared between garments, and no garment is missing from any view. Therefore, all three garments are consistently present across all views.\n\n---\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tracks\": [\n    {\n      \"garment\": \"maroon crewneck sweater\",\n      \"regions\": [\"R001\", \"R004\", \"R007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"garment\": \"grey crewneck sweater\",\n      \"regions\": [\"R002\", \"R005\", \"R008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"garment\": \"black-and-white checkered pants\",\n      \"regions\": [\"R003\", \"R006\", \"R009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
