      "raw": "After analyzing all three views (v0, v1, v2), we observe that each view contains the same three garments: a pair of black-and-white checkered trousers, a heather grey crewneck sweater, and a burgundy crewneck sweater. The only difference between the views is the arrangement of the garments in the original photograph.\n\nIn each view, the available mask regions (R001\u2013R009) correspond to these three garments, with each garment appearing in exactly three regions \u2014 one per view \u2014 and the regions are consistently assigned to the same garment across all views.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- **R001 (v2), R004 (v1), R007 (v0)** \u2192 all show the **black-and-white checkered trousers**.\n- **R002 (v2), R005 (v1), R008 (v0)** \u2192 all show the **heather grey crewneck sweater**.\n- **R003 (v2), R006 (v1), R009 (v0)** \u2192 all show the **burgundy crewneck sweater**.\n\nEach garment is visible in every view, and the mask regions for each garment are consistent in appearance (fabric, color, construction) across all views. There are no mixed masks or ambiguous regions. Each region corresponds to a single, distinct garment, and each garment is represented in every view.\n\nThus, we can form three tracks, one for each garment, using the corresponding region IDs from each view.\n\n---\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tracks\": [\n    {\n      \"garment\": \"black-and-white checkered trousers\",\n      \"regions\": [\"R001\", \"R004\", \"R007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"garment\": \"heather grey crewneck sweater\",\n      \"regions\": [\"R002\", \"R005\", \"R008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"garment\": \"burgundy crewneck sweater\",\n      \"regions\": [\"R003\", \"R006\", \"R009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
